    {
      "name": "exclamation_count",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A generic punctuation count with weak connection to product attributes; more a tone proxy than an attribute axis. Values (0 in all samples) are correctly extracted from text."
    },
    {
      "name": "positive_words_count",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
      "source_groundedness": 0.65,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Sentiment word counting is a mild tone axis, only loosely tied to product attributes. Grounding is shaky: the dog-food and confection reviews are clearly laudatory ('good quality', 'heaven', 'highly recommend') yet score 0 positive words, suggesting a narrow/unreliable lexicon."
    },
    {
      "name": "negative_words_count",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Negative sentiment count is a modest tone signal. The 'Not as Advertised' complaint scores 0 negative words, and the medicinal review scores 0, indicating the lexicon misses clearly negative content in these rows."
    },
    {
      "name": "helpfulness_ratio",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A clean numeric derivation from HelpfulnessNumerator/Denominator giving a real new axis. However it restates existing original columns rather than reading review text, so leverage for a product-attribute query is moderate. Values are correctly computed."
    },
    {
      "name": "sentiment_score",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Sentiment provides some analytical axis but here it is merely positive minus negative counts, largely redundant with those columns. Grounding suffers the same lexicon gaps: strongly positive reviews score 0."
    },
    {
      "name": "review_quality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.35,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "An opaque composite whose meaning is unclear; the values (0.67, 0.34, 1.0) are not obviously warranted by the source text (e.g. the negative peanut review scores 0.34 while a positive one scores 0.67, no clear mapping). Low transparency and questionable grounding."
    },
    {
      "name": "high_satisfaction",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.35,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A binarised satisfaction flag is almost entirely redundant with the original Score column and is essentially the target rather than an explanatory attribute. Values appear consistent with a Score threshold. Grounded but low added leverage."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.38,
  "source_groundedness": 0.72,
  "rationale": "The added columns are dominated by generic surface-text metrics (character/summary lengths, exclamation and sentiment-word counts) that give little leverage for identifying PRODUCT ATTRIBUTES associated with higher satisfaction; none capture actual product characteristics (category, flavor, packaging, price complaints, etc.). Several are mutually redundant (sentiment_score = positive - negative) and high_satisfaction merely restates the target Score. Grounding is generally acceptable for the mechanical counts and the helpfulness_ratio derivation, but the sentiment lexicon columns visibly miss clearly positive/negative content in multiple sampled rows, and review_quality is an opaque composite with no clear warrant from the source text. Overall modest usefulness and mixed groundedness."
}
